  "summary": "A University of Washington Center for Human Rights report found that eight Washington law enforcement agencies shared Flock ALPR data directly with ICE and Border Patrol, while ten others were accessed via 'backdoor' or 'side-door' channels. The article raises legal and civil liberties concerns about this sharing, including immigration and abortion-related risks.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "eight law enforcement agencies had shared information directly with federal agencies. The report named police departments in Arlington, Auburn, Lakewood, Richland, Sunnyside, Wenatchee, Yakima, and the Benton County Sheriff's Office as doing so as of 2025.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "Backdoor access allowed the agencies to obtain data even when local agencies had said they wouldn't share it.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "the sharing of local data is between users and their choice of whom they decide to share that information with.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
